## How Progress Is Tracked

- `{{PROGRESS_FILE}}` is the plan and progress table; the task breakdown lives in its rows. Edit it ONLY through `python3 {{GOAL_LOG}} <cmd>` (`--help` lists the commands), so rows never break on tab matching.
- This plan lives in this goal workspace; in worktree mode the workspace is on its own goal branch, and in dedicated-repo mode the repo itself is the ledger. Each loop step ends in a commit, so the commit history is the durable record. `goal_log.py done` stamps the current `HEAD` as the row checkpoint. On resume, recover state with `pwd`, `goal_log.py show`, `git log`, then re-run the Proof.

## Loop Protocol

Run as an autonomous loop until the Goal holds.

1. Read this file end to end, then `goal_log.py show` to see where you are. On the first turn and after any resume, bring up the Environment and run the fast Verification Layers once before touching a row, so a broken environment is found before it is blamed on a change.
2. Act as the orchestrator: select runnable rows whose dependencies are done, assign independent rows to workers, and keep `progress.tsv` a single-writer ledger. Run independent workers in separate worktrees; serialize overlapping or dependent rows.
3. For an independent row run `python3 {{GOAL_WORKER}} start <id> --worker <name> --type <type> --desc <kebab-description>`. The worker implements in that worktree and opens a PR against the goal branch titled `<type>(<scope>): <what changed> (#<issue>)`. Review the PR diff and the row's proof, then run `python3 {{GOAL_WORKER}} integrate <id> --pr <n> --head <reviewed-sha> --artifact "<proof>"`: it merges, records `done`, closes the issue, and removes the branch and worktree. Never run raw `git merge` or `gh pr merge` for a worker PR.
4. For behavior changes, first add or reproduce a failing test/check. Do the minimum work within Scope and Constraints, run the layers gated `every row` and the Proof, read the result, and fix the root cause until it passes or a real blocker is recorded. A step that blows its time budget is recorded as a failure; move on.
5. Record a row you did yourself with `goal_log.py done <id> --decision keep|discard|crash --artifact "<proof>"`. Record a user-only or external-condition hold with `goal_log.py defer <id> --reason "<why>" --resume-when "<condition>"`; record a technical dependency with `goal_log.py block <id> --notes "<why>"`. Deferred rows keep their issue, branch, and worktree while independent rows continue.
6. Commit those rows' files plus `{{PROGRESS_FILE}}`: `git add <files> {{PROGRESS_FILE}} && git commit -m "<type>(<scope>): <what you did> (#<issue>)"`. A worker row needs no extra commit; `integrate` made it. If target code changes live outside this repo, also commit the target repo or commit patch/snapshot artifacts here, according to Target Change Tracking.
7. Surface the Proof in your reply as evidence, not a claim: paste the actual command output (pass/fail, line numbers, exact errors), then name the checkpoint, what you verified this step, what remains, and whether you are deferred or blocked. The Proof must verify every Acceptance Criteria item. The `/goal` evaluator reads the conversation, not the files.
8. To undo a change that made things worse, revert with git, sparingly. Do not `git reset` away failed attempts you have already committed -- the commit log is the full record, including discards.
9. When the Goal holds, open the result PR with `goal_pr.py` as GitHub Flow describes and apply the merge policy.

Do not ask "should I continue?" once the loop starts. Defer only the affected row for real external sending, payment, publication, deployment, credentials, permission changes, irreversible external deletion, or a user-only choice. Stop only when the Goal holds, when you hit a Bound, or when no runnable row remains. Budget or turn exhaustion is not completion.

## Completion

Complete only when the Goal holds and is shown with the Proof output in the conversation or logs, required rows in `{{PROGRESS_FILE}}` are `done` or intentionally `dropped`, and the result PR is open with its URL in the reply.
